  "reason": "The records collectively make the later study plausibly reconstructible. R1 and R13 provide temperature-dependent molecular-dynamics deformation results in a closely similar FCC Cantor-type HEA, including decreasing dislocation density at high temperature, increased HCP fraction near 1000 K, stacking-fault evolution, and associated loss or alteration of strength. R2, R5, and R14 connect elevated-temperature mechanical behavior in similar HEAs to dislocation density and configurations, while R10 directly links HCP formation to stacking faults. R12 supplies elevated-temperature film tribology, and R18 supplies nanoindentation-based hardness and modulus measurement. Thus an expert could reasonably combine temperature-controlled indentation/friction testing with analysis of dislocations, stacking faults, HCP atoms, and lattice disorder. The precise FeNiCrCoCu-on-Cu geometry, simulation speeds and depths, and the exact 600–900 K crossover are not disclosed, but proposing the overall intervention and mechanism would not require a substantial new conceptual idea."
